1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.300
You're now ready to start building
your agent.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.000
You're given a fake resume that you will
use to fill out a fake job application.

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.533
In this lesson, you will set up
the RAG capability of your agent.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.300
You will use Llama parse to parse the resume,

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.833
load the extracted information
into a vector store,

6
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.400
and then run basic queries to test it.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.033
All right, let's go.

8
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.000
You need nested
async for this notebook to work.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
So, let's enable it here.

10
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.233
First we bring in our imports

11
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:32.233
and then we bring in nested async.

12
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.300
And we execute both of the cells
just to get them ready.

13
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.200
We're also going to need our API keys.

14
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.000
OpenAI we used earlier.

15
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.800
And we're also going to need a Llama
cloud API key

16
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.600
because we're going to be using Llama parse
to parse the PDFs. Llama parce

17
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.300
is an advanced document parser
that can read PDFs,

18
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.300
word files, PowerPoints,
Excel spreadsheets, stuff like that.

19
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.000
It's really great at getting information
out of complicated PDFs into a form

20
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.033
LLMs find easy to understand.

21
00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:01.600
You can get a key at cloud
dot LlamaIndex.AI for free.

22
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.500
Let's start by parsing a resume.

23
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.700
One of the coolest features of Llama
Parse is the ability to tell it

24
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.533
what kind of document it's parsing,
so that it will more intelligently parse

25
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:12.633
the contents.

26
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:15.233
In this case, you can tell it
that it's reading a resume.

27
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:17.400
So let's

28
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.400
import Llama Parse and then let's pass
a document.

29
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.400
You do this by calling Llama Parse

30
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.500
passing in your API key telling it
what result type you want.

31
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:29.000
This could be markdown or text
or a number of other types.

32
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.800
And then we're going to give it a content
guideline instruction

33
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.600
saying: this is a resume.

34
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.100
Gather related facts together and format
it as bullet points with headers.

35
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:41.033
Once you've created a parser on parser,
you can call load data.

36
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.033
With our fake resume,
it starts parsing the file

37
00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:46.600
depending on the size of the file,

38
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.900
this can take a little while,

39
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.000
but our resume is pretty short,
so it shouldn't take too long.

40
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.900
Once it's done, we can print

41
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.700
what we get back from the parser,
which is an array of documents.

42
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.100
So in this case, we're going to take

43
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.533
the third element of the array
and print out the text of that document.

44
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:05.100
You can see that
it's passed out some projects

45
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:09.200
and said
what's in the resume with headers.

46
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.000
Remember it's doing markdown.

47
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.800
So it's got a project's header.

48
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.600
It's got a company name.

49
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.500
It's got bullet points saying what

50
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.500
our fake candidate
did and all of those jobs.

51
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.033
Now that we've got our documents,
we can pass in to our vector store index.

52
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.600
The vector store
index will embed the text,

53
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.400
meaning it'll turn it into vectors
that we can search.

54
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.000
To do this,
it needs to use an embedding API.

55
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.000
We'll be using one provided by OpenAI,
which is why we needed an OpenAI key

56
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:36.800
for this.

57
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.233
So here we've imported OpenAI's embeddings
and our vector store index.

58
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.900
And now we're going to create an index
using vector store index.

59
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.733
Vector store index has a from documents
method

60
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.733
that takes the documents
that were returned by Llama Parse.

61
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:53.000
And here we're passing
in the embedding model.

62
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.900
Just to be absolutely clear
what embeddings we should use.

63
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.333
We're using text embedding through small,
which is a very good embedding model

64
00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:00.500
provided by OpenAI.

65
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.800
Now once again we're going

66
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.800
to bring in our OpenAI LLM.

67
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.300
And we're going to instantiate it.

68
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:13.300
Again we're going to use the

69
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.533
GPT-4o-mini model
because it's quick and it's cheap.

70
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.100
Once you've got an index,

71
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.833
you can create a query engine
using the as query engine method.

72
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.500
You pass in which LLM
you want your query engine to use,

73
00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:29.433
and you pass in similarity
top K when your query engine

74
00:03:29.433 --> 00:03:32.100
is retrieving context
to answer the question,

75
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.500
it will rank
all of the content that it finds

76
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.200
by how relevant it is to your query,
rather than return all of it.

77
00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:42.500
It only returns a top number.

78
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.500
In this case, it's
going to return the top five.

79
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.033
Once we've got our query engine set up,
we can call query with a question.

80
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.600
A really basic question.

81
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.600
In this case, what is their name
and what was their most recent job.

82
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.900
And then we can print out the response.

83
00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.000
Our fake developer's name is Sarah Chen.

84
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.533
And their most recent job is senior
full Stack developer at TechFlow Solutions.

85
00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:05.633
One thing that you might need to do

86
00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:08.600
is store vector Store,
so that you can use it later.

87
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.333
Vector stores are easily
persisted to disk.

88
00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:14.500
You define a storage directory
in which you want to store your data,

89
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.400
and you call the persist method.

90
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.800
That's all you need to do.

91
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.733
It's now stored on your disk.

92
00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:23.533
To load it back, you need a new method
load index from storage

93
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.600
and a new class the storage context.

94
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.700
Here, you're going to check whether or not
you've already run this notebook.

95
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.533
If you've already run this notebook,
then the storage directory will exist

96
00:04:34.533 --> 00:04:39.100
and your content will already be there,
in which case you'll load storage context

97
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.000
from defaults,
giving it the same persistence directory

98
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.300
that you used earlier,

99
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.733
and you'll create your index
by calling load index from storage.

100
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.733
Otherwise
it will say that it didn't find it.

101
00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:55.033
We already
called persist, so that just worked.

102
00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:59.233
Now that we've got our restored index,
we can call it with as query

103
00:04:59.233 --> 00:05:02.233
engine dot query again,
just like we did before.

104
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.233
Again, it knows that our fake developers
name is Sarah Chen.

105
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:07.600
Congratulations.

106
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.633
What you've done is you've performed
retrieval augmented generation on a resume

107
00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:14.900
document. RAG is a very powerful technique
that with proper

108
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.900
scaling, can work across databases
of thousands of documents.

109
00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:20.200
With our RAG pipeline in hand,

110
00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.933
let's turn it into a tool that can be used
by an agent to answer questions.

111
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.133
This is a stepping stone

112
00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.433
towards creating an agentic system
that can perform your larger goal.

113
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.300
We'll need two new classes for this:
a function tool and a function

114
00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.300
calling agent.

115
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.800
The first step is to create
a regular Python function

116
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.533
that performs a RAG query.

117
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:41.333
It's important to give this function
a descriptive name to markets, inputs,

118
00:05:41.333 --> 00:05:44.000
and output types,
and to include a docstring

119
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.700
that is the thing in triple quotes
that you're about to see.

120
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.733
This describes what it does.

121
00:05:48.733 --> 00:05:52.500
The framework will give all of this
metadata to the LLM, the name,

122
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.100
the types, and the docstring and the LLM
will use that metadata to decide

123
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.600
whether or not this tool is useful
and how to use this tool.

124
00:06:00.633 --> 00:06:02.300
Once we've defined our function,

125
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.600
we turn it into a tool that can be used
by an agent using the from defaults

126
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.600
function, a function tool,
and we pass in the function.

127
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.500
Now you can instantiate a function
calling agent that uses this tool.

128
00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:16.733
There are a number of different
agent types supported by the LlamaIndex.

129
00:06:16.733 --> 00:06:17.833
This one is particularly

130
00:06:17.833 --> 00:06:20.700
capable and efficient
and it works well with OpenAI.

131
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.600
To create it,
you pass in an array of tools.

132
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:24.200
Just one

133
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.200
in this case, you pass it the LLM
that it should use to power the agent.

134
00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.500
And in this case
we're going to set verbose equals true

135
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.900
so that we can see what it's doing
when it works.

136
00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.700
Now that we've created our agent
we can chat with it and ask it a question.

137
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.800
In this case we're going to ask it
how many years of experience

138
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.100
the applicant has.

139
00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.100
You can see the output being very noisy
because we've set

140
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.700
verbose is equal to true.

141
00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.833
So it tells us when it's running steps.

142
00:06:48.833 --> 00:06:52.333
It says that it's added this user
message to memory, which is our question.

143
00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.500
And then you can see it's
calling the function.

144
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.500
You can see which function it's calling,
query resume.

145
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.833
And you can see what parameters
it's passing in.

146
00:06:59.833 --> 00:07:02.933
The query is how many years of experience
does the applicant have.

147
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.533
This is the output
that it got from the function.

148
00:07:05.533 --> 00:07:08.300
And this is what the LLM responded with.

149
00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:12.200
You see it twice because verbose
printed it once and we printed it once.

150
00:07:12.533 --> 00:07:15.800
Now you've got a RAG pipeline
and an agent.

151
00:07:15.933 --> 00:07:16.833
Let's wrap them up

152
00:07:16.833 --> 00:07:20.300
neatly into a workflow
that you can extend in your later lessons.

153
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.900
You won't rely on any of the things
you've already created in this lesson.

154
00:07:24.233 --> 00:07:26.733
You will start this one from scratch.

155
00:07:26.733 --> 00:07:31.000
So just like we did in the last lesson,
we're going to import our events

156
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.400
and our other workflow related
core classes.

157
00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.300
You'll see
start event and stop event, steps,

158
00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.300
event and context,
all of which you've seen before.

159
00:07:39.433 --> 00:07:41.200
We're going to define our query event,

160
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.433
which is the only event that we need
in this particular workflow so far.

161
00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.700
And now we're going to create a RAG
workflow.

162
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.400
This is pretty complicated.

163
00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.333
So let's step through it line by line.
A RAG workflow is a class.

164
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:57.300
So you can give it class variables
just like any other class.

165
00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.200
It's got a storage directory.

166
00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.900
We've given it an LLM
and we've given it a query engine.

167
00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.400
These are the types here.

168
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.300
The first step is going to be set up.

169
00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.700
We're going to check that
we've been given a resume file

170
00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.300
which is attached to the start event.

171
00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.233
And we're going to instantiate
our LLM as OpenAI

172
00:08:17.233 --> 00:08:19.700
again. This section you've seen before.

173
00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:23.033
We're going to check
if our storage signature already exists.

174
00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:25.333
And if it does
we're going to load it from disk.

175
00:08:25.333 --> 00:08:28.500
If it doesn't, then we're going to parse
and load our documents.

176
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
This is exactly the same thing
that we did earlier in the lesson.

177
00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.300
We then create a vector store index
just like we did earlier in the lesson,

178
00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.933
and we persist it to disk
just like we did earlier in the lesson.

179
00:08:40.033 --> 00:08:44.100
Either way, you've now got an index
and you turned it into a query engine.

180
00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.700
Now, you've got a query engine.

181
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.300
You can fire off the query event.

182
00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.700
The query event

183
00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.700
will trigger ask question
and ask a question will simply query

184
00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.700
the query engine with a question
about the applicant.

185
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.800
So, let's instantiate that workflow

186
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.000
and we'll run it
just like we've run any other workflow.

187
00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.700
In this case,
the workflow is expecting two parameters.

188
00:09:10.733 --> 00:09:13.733
One is the location of the resume file
that it can parse,

189
00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.300
and one is the query about the applicant.

190
00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.533
As you can see,
it worked very quickly because

191
00:09:20.533 --> 00:09:22.533
we'd already persisted this to disk.

192
00:09:22.533 --> 00:09:24.400
It didn't need to reparse the document.

193
00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.900
It was able to just immediately
answer the question.

194
00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.500
There's nothing in this workflow
that you haven't done before.

195
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.200
It's just making things neat
and encapsulated. If you're

196
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.833
particularly suspicious, you might note
that there's a small bug here.

197
00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.200
If you run this a second time
with a different resume,

198
00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.833
this code will find
the old resume on disk,

199
00:09:41.833 --> 00:09:44.700
and therefore it won't bother to parse
the new resume.

200
00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.933
You don't need to fix that now,
but think about how you might fix that.

201
00:09:48.233 --> 00:09:49.033
Congratulations!

202
00:09:49.033 --> 00:09:52.033
Once you've successfully created
an agent with the RAG tools.

203
00:09:52.333 --> 00:09:55.500
In the next lesson, you'll give your agent
some more complicated tasks.

